[Music]  hi my name's Dave Chang were at my  restaurant majordomo here in North  Chinatown Los Angeles the term ugly  delicious came about sort of as not as a  joke but something I think I've been  turning around with for many years that  good food could be really great even  though it doesn't look so pretty just  because something looks imperfect  doesn't mean that it's not perfect right  and it's really not only beauty and the  eye of the beholder but it's like who's  setting these standards quite frankly  the ugly delicious at its core though is  to sort of be accepting of many  different things and understanding that  multiple truths might be true and  ultimately know that there's a hidden  value in certain things right you may  not like it right off the bat but the  more you're patient with it the more you  understand it you might find it to be  more delicious than you originally  thought  Koreans called bread Fung Chinese call  it Bing so there's a lot of words in  China Japan Korea that have very similar  associations and having spent time and  traveled throughout those regions and  even a lot of bread I just thought well  this is wildly not misrepresented and I  think misunderstood if you look at this  you're gonna be like oh it's just like a  flatbread I think the next story for me  is like how do you make the flatbread  what is it similar to and like how do  you consume it because you could stuff  it you could just tear it off and dip it  in something I mean it's just bread man  it's griddle bread we're gonna make some  water and we're gonna put in some yeast  and flour you change the recipe with  some sugar sometimes some salt we're  gonna pour [ __ ] out probably like a  hundred grams bench it out proof it and  then roll it out again and then put it  on the griddle probably it's like 350  degrees and you're cooking it like a  pancake long story cut short it's just  really delicious and it allows us to  serve it with this thing called chickpea  hosana basically a chickpea dip but  we're not making it like traditional  hummus it's way more Asian if not  anything more Amer  can we ferment chickpeas with Koji which  is Aspergillus oryzae which is very  popular right now but is the engine that  drives much of I would say Japanese for  many Goods I'm mixing in lemon juice and  olive oil and some sesame oil maybe a  little girl and we garnish it with  sesame seeds and a little chili sesame  oil that's a super simple process but  it's actually very very hard to get  right I think with majordomo we wanted  to do something that was out of our  comfort zone a little bit I think  there's some obviously DNA from what  we've done in the past but we really  wanted to celebrate what it might mean  to be a Los Angeles restaurant and I  think for us we were trying to think if  this was our only restaurant what would  that look like and this is it so I think  it's a celebration of all things  California and I think if you look at  California cuisine today this really is  I think a continuation of what Wolfgang  said years ago which is like it's a  melting pot and our food here should  represent that three Michelin star  restaurants are fantastic but you  shouldn't only celebrate those  restaurants utterly delicious is  challenging conventional wisdom

